{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'pwd && git status --short && cat AGENTS.md && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'pwd && git status --short && cat AGENTS.md && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-yang-s9\n?? collab/1793288816996-engineer-yang-slice9.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793288816996-engineer-yang-slice9.prompt.md\n# ==============================================================================\n# datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\n# ==============================================================================\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793128583916-architect-yang-latte-gap-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793128583916-architect-yang-latte-gap-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-Local: 2026-10-28 02:25:06 ICT\n\nThe recommended path is **reusable compiler corrections plus an explicit LaTTe portability layer**, verified against the original kernel running on Clojure. Compilation success alone is not a landing criterion. No files were edited.\n\nArchitectural decisions below are binding recommendations; helper names, file layout, and equivalent lowering implementations remain implementer choices.\n\n**Verified findings that change the plan**\n\n- **Ast-walker tail calls are iterative without consulting `:tail?`.** Both its stepped evaluator and hot loop enter a closure body with the application-evaluation frame removed. Pending enclosing computations remain. At 100 and 1,000 recursive iterations, measured maximum continuation depth stayed at **2**. Removing tail flags made no difference. See [walker application transitions](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:303).\n- **The semantic VM depends on correct tail flags.** Its maximum return-stack depth was **0** for the same programs; removing flags produced **102 frames at 100 iterations**. Both hot loops completed 10,000 iterations. Projection preserves the flag, analysis treats tail calls as terminators, and runtime avoids pushing a return frame. See [semantic invocation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:190).\n- **Both lanes have Clojure truthiness:** only `nil` and `false` were falsy; `0`, `\"\"`, `[]`, `{}`, `#{}`, and `()` were truthy. However, [compile-if](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:228) incorrectly turns an explicit `false` alternate into `nil`. This was reproduced.\n- **Additional runtime gaps are real:** docstrings, multi-arity functions, `defn-`, standard control macros, callable collections, record predicates, metadata operations, and the `try/catch` in `typing/unfold-implicit`. Yin deliberately nil-fills missing closure arguments and drops extras; changing that globally would break an existing [VM contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:55).\n- **Collection shape matters.** LaTTe distinguishes list-shaped binders from vector-shaped applications. Existing VM `rest` returns a vector. A blanket “sequences become vectors” policy is therefore unsafe.\n- **The oracle must be upstream execution.** The spike changed `utils/pair?` from `(vector v)` to `(vector? v)`. Upstream returns `true` for `'(1 2)`; the adjusted implementation does not. Its vector-only examples missed this difference.\n\nThe probes used Clojure **1.12.4**, Java **21.0.2**, and LaTTe commit **`86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279`**. These were focused probes, not a regression-suite run.\n\n1. **Freeze the oracle, admitted subset, and portability boundary**\n\n   **Approach:** Create a small, checked-in manifest covering the reported 143 definitions, their source locations, arities, required forms, dependencies, and disposition: unchanged, mechanically lowered, explicitly adapted, or excluded. Read namespaces with their aliases and reader conditionals intact; preserve qualified symbols and auto-resolved keywords.\n\n   Use three executions:\n\n   - **A:** original pinned LaTTe on direct Clojure.\n   - **B:** the explicit portability adaptation on direct Clojure.\n   - **C:** that adaptation compiled through Yang and executed on each VM lane.\n\n   A↔B validates adaptation; B↔C isolates compiler/runtime defects. Neither comparison substitutes for the other.\n\n   Establish a module-local calling convention for the portable library: functions receive an argument vector and explicit context, and return a tagged success/error outcome with the resulting context. Lower this entirely to existing lambdas, applications, conditionals, and data. Existing Yang/VM callers use explicit boundary adapters; their native calling convention remains intact.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Reproduce upstream anchors for `utils/vcons`, `utils/pair?`, `syntax/alpha-norm`, and `nbe/norm`. Account for every manifest entry and every admitted free symbol. Unsupported forms must fail admission, rather than survive as unresolved applications.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Establish baselines for `yang.clojure-test`, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test`, and the REPL frontend. The named spike stdout currently contains an earlier interrupted run; regenerate evidence instead of treating it as the definitive manifest.\n\n   **Risk:** Selecting only easy examples or comparing two identically rewritten programs can manufacture confidence.\n\n2. **Correct truthiness compilation and collection evaluation; establish data primitives**\n\n   **Approach:** Fix explicit `false` alternates immediately.\n\n   **Literal-divergence ruling: change Yang semantics.** Unquoted vectors, maps, and sets evaluate their constituent expressions. Quoted collections remain data. Do not preserve silent self-evaluation through a lint exception or a LaTTe-only workaround.\n\n   Retain literal AST nodes for recursively constant collections, preserving current constant-literal tests and addresses where possible. Dynamic collections lower to construction calls. Evaluate vector elements in order; evaluate each map key/value and set member exactly once, with traversal behavior pinned by oracle fixtures. Do not sort executable expressions merely to canonicalize data.\n\n   Add or qualify only the necessary callback-free primitives: collection constructors/accessors, predicates, counting, arithmetic helpers, symbol/keyword construction, text conversion, and metadata access. Publish their arity/effect profiles.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Differential fixtures for nested dynamic collections, symbol lookup, quoted equivalents, evaluated map keys, duplicate evaluated keys/members, and evaluation-once behavior. Direct examples include `(let [x 7] [x])`, `{x (+ x 1)}`, and `#{x}`. LaTTe anchors: `defenv/mkenv`, syntax predicates, and collection-shaped return values.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Existing literal tests, set round trips, metadata/provenance tests, and the four-VM stream/REPL corpus. Search consumers for unquoted collections containing symbols/forms; migrate intentional data to `quote`.\n\n   **Risk:** This intentionally changes previously incorrect behavior. Newly executed expressions may perform effects. That requires an explicit compatibility note and consumer audit, not weaker semantics.\n\n3. **Function headers, sequential destructuring, and Clojure call adapters**\n\n   **Approach:** Support the corpus’s docstrings, metadata, `defn-`, fixed/multiple arities, and required variadic library functions. Keep `defmacro` behavior and user-macro precedence unchanged.\n\n   Lower sequential destructuring hygienically. For example:\n\n   ```clojure\n   [a b & r :as whole]\n   ```\n\n   becomes a single evaluated temporary followed by bindings equivalent to:\n\n   ```clojure\n   a     (nth temporary 0 nil)\n   b     (nth temporary 1 nil)\n   r     (nthnext temporary 2)\n   whole temporary\n   ```\n\n   Apply this recursively for nested sequential patterns. Function parameters become fresh simple binders followed by the same expansion. `:as` preserves the original value; exhausted `&` tails must follow Clojure’s nil/sequence behavior.\n\n   In the portable module, lower multi-arity functions to argument-count dispatch inside an ordinary guest closure. All calls use the same guest invocation helper. That helper also implements the admitted keyword/map/set/vector invocation semantics. Native primitive adapters unpack arguments and call callback-free primitives directly.\n\n   This avoids changing `engine/bind-params`, adding lambda grammar slots, or making every map a VM-callable object.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Missing elements, nil inputs, nested patterns, `&`, `:as`, shadowing, initializer dependencies, and malformed patterns. Distinguish missing arguments from explicit nil and reject wrong Clojure arities. LaTTe anchors: `syntax/top-assumption` and `defenv/register-definition`, including both arities and local/global placement.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Existing lambda/let tests, native under-/over-arity tests, macro-shadowing tests, nested `defn`, and top-level redefinition behavior. Test calls crossing explicit module adapters.\n\n   **Risk:** Accidental capture, argument loss, or indiscriminate rewriting of shadowed core names. Generated bindings need an explicit fresh-name supply and retained source provenance.\n\n4. **Lower `loop/recur` through lexical self-application**\n\n   **Approach:** Compile a loop to a private worker closure with an explicit self parameter and loop parameters. A recurrence becomes a tail application of that worker to itself and the next values. No generated global definition, mutable cell, or host trampoline is needed.\n\n   Initializers run sequentially, with earlier bindings visible. Recur operands all evaluate against the old bindings before rebinding. Destructuring occurs again at each iteration.\n\n   Track the **source recurrence target** separately from generated lambda boundaries. `recur` targets the nearest source loop or function arity, never an incidental lambda introduced by `let` or `do`. Reject wrong arity, non-tail recurrence, and crossing a source function boundary.\n\n   Audit every generated lambda body for tail positions. The existing Z-combinator helper has unmarked applications and must not be reused without correction.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Oracle agreement for nested loops, simultaneous swaps, zero iterations, function-level recur, destructured bindings, and loops used as non-tail operands. LaTTe anchors: `utils/vconcat`, `utils/vcons`, `typing/ctx-fetch`, `syntax/binderify`, `syntax/appify`, and `syntax/assumptions`.\n\n   Require bounded continuation depth as iteration counts increase on **both** lanes, including after later error/state lowering.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Native recursive definitions, non-tail recursive controls, semantic analysis/frame validation, and the stream pump/drain programs.\n\n   **Risk:** The walker can conceal missing tail flags that leak frames on the semantic VM. Conversely, a falsely marked tail call can discard required work.\n\n5. **Make errors explicit before adding more control constructs**\n\n   **Approach:** Use tagged result propagation in the portable adaptation. `ex-info` constructs portable error data; `throw` produces an error outcome and prevents subsequent computation. An error is never an ordinary truthy return value.\n\n   Preserve LaTTe’s existing `[:ok …]`/`[:ko …]` values *inside* the outer execution outcome; they are application results, not automatically exceptions.\n\n   Adapt `typing/unfold-implicit`’s actual catch site explicitly: consume the implicit function’s error outcome and reproduce the original message/data merge and `[:ko … nil]` result. Do not implement general JVM exception classes, stack traces, or arbitrary `try/finally`.\n\n   Primitive adapters must translate declared domain failures consistently. Unexpected VM/compiler failures remain harness failures, never successful LaTTe rejection.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** `utils/safe-get` success and failure, errors inside operands and nested calls, and proof that later operands/effects do not execute. Later gates cover `presyntax/parse`, `typing/type-of`, and both success/failure paths through `unfold-implicit`. Inventory all 57 reported throw sites and assign fixtures or explicit deferred status.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Existing VM/REPL error rendering and recovery remain unchanged outside the portable adapter. Re-run tail-depth tests: forwarding an outcome in tail position must not accumulate unwrap/rewrap continuations.\n\n   **Risk:** Swallowing implementation failures as proof rejection, or continuing execution after an error. Nonportable error payloads require explicit adaptation; never silently discard them.\n\n6. **Lower `case` to evaluate-once conditional dispatch**\n\n   **Approach:** Bind the scrutinee once and generate nested `if` comparisons against literal constants. Symbol constants such as `λ` and `Π` are data, not variable references. Preserve branch tail positions and evaluate only the selected branch.\n\n   Validate duplicate constants and malformed cases. A missing default raises the defined error outcome on no match. Support the corpus’s constant domain; broader numeric dispatch behavior needs its own oracle coverage.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Every constant/default arm from all six source sites gets a minimal dispatch fixture. `syntax/mk-fresh` must match upstream across suffix levels and forbidden-name collisions. Full `typing/type-of-term`, normalization-mode, and proof-step dispatch gates land when their dependencies become available.\n\n   **Regression gate:** `if`, `and`, `or`, short-circuit effects, and semantic tail-path validation.\n\n   **Risk:** Re-evaluating the scrutinee, evaluating symbol constants, or returning nil for an unmatched case.\n\n7. **Build the guest collection and higher-order library**\n\n   **Approach:** Keep all guest-function invocation inside Yin execution.\n\n   | Facility | Decision |\n   |---|---|\n   | `map`, `mapv`, `filter`, `remove`, `reduce` | Guest loops invoking guest closures |\n   | `into`, `concat`, `merge`, set union/difference, required update helpers | Guest library over data primitives |\n   | `comp`, `partial` | Guest closure construction |\n   | `apply` | Guest argument assembly followed by the same invocation helper |\n   | `str` | Callback-free conversion/concatenation primitives with guest variadic assembly |\n   | `symbol`, `keyword` | Profiled, callback-free data primitives |\n   | `gensym` | Explicit fresh-name supply, integrated with context threading |\n   | `lazy-seq` | Unsupported generally; explicit finite adaptation of `utils/zip` |\n\n   Under the argument-vector convention, `apply` can invoke closures without a new VM opcode or host callback: assemble the final argument vector, then call the guest entry closure. Primitive functions are represented by compatible guest adapters.\n\n   Use eager finite sequences where justified, while preserving list/sequence versus vector distinctions. `mapv` returns vectors; list construction and list-target `into` retain their behavior. Keep the existing native VM `rest` contract; the portable library supplies Clojure-compatible sequence operations.\n\n   Support `reduce` early termination: `presyntax/parse-terms` uses `reduced`. Do not substitute host `Reduced` objects into portable data.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Empty and nonempty reductions, supported multi-collection mapping, argument order, captured closures, `apply` with prefix arguments, and higher-order functions passed through collections. LaTTe anchors: `utils/map-with`, `utils/vremove`, `syntax/term-reduce`, `free-vars`, `vars`, `subst`, `alpha-norm`, and `readable-term`.\n\n   For `zip`, compare fully realized finite outputs and odd-length failure. Admit the eager rewrite only after checking its consumers do not depend on partial realization or exception timing.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Closure ownership/serialization checks, primitive profiles, sequence predicates, metadata preservation, and both VM lanes. Callback functions used in these fixtures must be guest code.\n\n   **Risk:** Host callbacks, reversed list accumulation, lost symbol metadata, and eager evaluation changing failure timing. Ordinary Clojure `=` alone is insufficient to detect every shape/metadata difference.\n\n8. **Replace ambient LaTTe state and host representations**\n\n   **Approach:** Introduce an immutable kernel context containing definition configuration, unparser registry, opacity policy, unfold-implicit binding, and fresh-name supply. State-changing operations return an updated context; callers thread it explicitly.\n\n   The two unfold-implicit atoms represent one installation relationship: initialize the shared binding explicitly. Preserve unparser category/order and replacement behavior. Opacity operations must return updated definitions and propagate them through subsequent proof steps; replacing only the atom while leaving `resolve`/Var mutation would not solve the problem.\n\n   Keep general `defrecord` out. Adapt the five known LaTTe record kinds to tagged data, with explicit constructors/predicates. Replace host namespace/Var fallback with the supplied definitional environment. Guest extension functions are compiled closures, never raw Clojure callbacks.\n\n   Separate timing and pretty-printing from checker semantics. Requested diagnostic output uses explicit stream effects; no hidden printing or wall-clock primitive enters conversion.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** Two independent contexts remain isolated; prior contexts retain their values; install/uninstall and opacity transitions match equivalent upstream operation sequences. Cover transparent/opaque definitions, axioms, proved/unproved theorems, notation functions, and implicit functions.\n\n   LaTTe anchors: `defenv/fetch-definition`, `nbe/delta-reduction`, `typing/unfold-implicit`, unparser registration/unregistration, and opacity elaboration.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Context replay, closure-containing registry reconstruction, plain code-data validation, module isolation, and the store-write audit. Runtime closure values must not be embedded as literal host functions.\n\n   **Risk:** Incomplete state propagation, residual namespace lookup, or comparing adapted maps against upstream records without checking their kind and fields.\n\n9. **Close the kernel-level differential gate**\n\n   **Approach:** Assemble the admitted dependency graph and execute real parsing, substitution, normalization, typing, and proof checking end to end.\n\n   **Acceptance criteria:** At minimum:\n\n   - `presyntax/parse`: valid binders/applications and malformed inputs.\n   - `syntax/subst`: capture avoidance and shadowing.\n   - `nbe/norm`, `norm/normalize`, `norm/beta-eq?`: beta/delta reduction and opacity.\n   - `typing/type-of-term`, `type-of`, `type-check?`: valid and invalid terms.\n   - `proof/compile-proof`, `elab-proof`, `check-proof`: accepted proofs, rejected proofs, incomplete scripts, and steps after `qed`.\n   - Unparser and implicit-extension fixtures using guest closures.\n\n   Every admitted function/arity must be exercised directly or through a named integration fixture. No unexplained mismatch, unsupported reachable dependency, or silently skipped negative case may remain.\n\n   **Regression gate:** Run the Yang/stream/REPL regression set, semantic projection/analysis/frame suites, and affected cross-host lanes. Record JVM, Node, and Dart separately; an unavailable or failing lane is not green. Existing four-evaluator REPL behavior remains a regression obligation even though the primary oracle gate targets walker and semantic execution.\n\n   **Risk:** Mistaking LaTTe conformance for CIC soundness. This gate validates the execution substrate and port; it does not establish the different logical specification planned for `yin.cic`.\n\n**Oracle sampling and evidence discipline**\n\nBorrow the [PHP oracle discipline](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1235), using three compact fixture sources:\n\n- Upstream LaTTe tests selected by a checked-in supported-surface predicate.\n- Minimal anchors for each lowering rule, primitive contract, and error branch.\n- Bounded generated terms and operation sequences with recorded seeds; fixed samples in the quick tier and larger samples in the slow tier.\n\nSample the 143 functions by **dependency and semantic feature**, not randomly. Include every namespace, required arity, binding shape, higher-order call shape, and state/error boundary. Maintain a cumulative manifest showing exactly what each slice newly covers.\n\nCompare result tags, values, meaningful collection kinds, required metadata, errors, resulting context, and effect order. Normalize only declared differences: record representation and identified fresh-name correspondence, for example. Preserve free-variable identity and freshness relationships; do not blanket-alpha-normalize every output or coerce all sequences to vectors.\n\nEach slice records source/runtime pins, compiler and VM revisions, adaptation version, selected fixture IDs and counts, exact invocation, A/B/C outcomes, approved departures, and relevant continuation-depth measurements. A failure yields a small reproducible fixture. Golden updates require a fresh oracle result.\n\n**Scope boundaries**\n\nNo general macro-system changes, arbitrary host macro expansion, map destructuring, named functions, protocols, multimethods, general records, Java interop, namespace/Var semantics, unrestricted lazy sequences, transducers, or concurrent atoms. Admit only the standard-form expansions actually needed by the corpus, with tests and explicit rejection elsewhere.\n\nFor CIC, carry forward evaluated collections, hygienic binding, verified tail lowering, guest higher-order execution, explicit state, and data errors. Author the new checker directly against that restricted contract instead of inheriting LaTTe’s host-specific compatibility adaptations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
